<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khana Ratsadon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1927">1927</a>–<a href="/years/1933">1933</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as People's Party (Siam)</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Khana Ratsadon, or People's Party, was a group of civilian and military officials founded by Thai students in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1927. Led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pridi-banomyong">Pridi Banomyong</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plaek-phibunsongkhram">Plaek Phibunsongkhram</a>, it carried out the bloodless revolution of 24 June 1932 that ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absolute-monarchy">absolute monarchy</a> under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prajadhipok">Prajadhipok</a> and introduced Siam's first constitution. Its members dominated Thai politics for the next fifteen years.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khana-ratsadon.jpg" alt="Khana Ratsadon" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jo Shigeru · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Memorial_peg_of_Siamese_Revolution_of_2475.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prajadhipok">Prajadhipok</a> (Rama VII), the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absolute-monarchy">absolute monarchy</a> of the Chakri dynasty faced serious economic problems and threats from the British and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empires</a>, while the urban middle classes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a> grew and began to demand more rights. In February 1927 seven Siamese students met for five days at a hotel on the Rue Du Sommerard in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and founded the group. Among them were Lieutenant Plaek Khittasangkha, later Luang Phibulsonggram, a student at the School of Applied Artillery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pridi-banomyong">Pridi Banomyong</a>, a law student at the Institut d'Études Politiques de Paris, whom they made their president.</p><p>Calling themselves the promoters, they adopted Six Principles: the supreme power of the Thai people, national security, economic welfare, equality, rights and liberties, and public education for all. To achieve them they resolved to overthrow the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absolute-monarchy">absolute monarchy</a>, using force if necessary. Back in Siam they recruited 102 members in four branches: civilians under Laung Praditmanutham, the navy under Group Commander Luang Sinthusongkhramchai, junior army officers under Major Luang Phibulsonggram and senior officers under Colonel Phraya Phahonphonphayuhasena.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>By 1933 the party had made Siam a single party state, and it produced six prime ministers from its ranks. In power it intensified assimilation of the Chinese community: laws of 1938 and 1939 barred Chinese from certain professions, trades and residential areas, and the Nationality Law was amended in 1939 to require those seeking citizenship to adopt Thai names, send their children to Thai schools, speak Thai and renounce allegiance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Factional rivalry split the party into a civilian wing under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pridi-banomyong">Pridi Banomyong</a> and a military wing under Marshal Pibulsongkram, and it declined at about the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Later governments played down the party's significance. Sala Chaloem Thai was demolished in 1989, and after the 2014 coup several landmarks linked to the party were removed without explanation. The last surviving member, Lieutenant Krachang Tularak, died on 24 June 2009.</p><p>A brass plaque marking the 1932 revolution in the Royal Plaza went missing in April 2017 and was replaced with one praising the Chakri dynasty, and in 2019 statues of Phraya Phahon and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plaek-phibunsongkhram">Plaek Phibunsongkhram</a> were removed from an army base in Lopburi Province renamed after King Bhumibol. Historians such as Chatri Prakitnonthakan called the removals ideological cleansing by the conservative establishment. During the 2020 protests young demonstrators set a new plaque in Sanam Luang, and one group called itself Khana Ratsadon 2563.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Khana Ratsadon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 24 June 1932 the <b>People’s Party</b> (Khana Ratsadon), a small group of civilian and military officials led by Phraya Phahon Phonphayuhasena, Pridi Banomyong and Plaek Phibunsongkhram, seized key points in Bangkok in a bloodless coup, detained leading princes and demanded a constitutional regime.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rattanakosin-kingdom">Rattanakosin Kingdom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>About five days after the coup of June 1932, Luang Nithetkolakit revealed that he was a member of the People's Party (<b>Khana Ratsadon</b>) and asked again, now on behalf of the new regime.</blockquote><a href="/songs/thai-national-anthem">Thai National Anthem</a></li><li><blockquote>The revolution of 24 June 1932 by the <b>Khana Ratsadon</b>, led by Pridi Banomyong and Plaek Phibunsongkhram, ended absolute monarchy in the Rattanakosin Kingdom and introduced a constitution.</blockquote><a href="/state/thailand">Thailand</a></li></ul></section>
